---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "stellar-characters-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <p class="outcome">
    <strong>Accepted · 11 November 2026.</strong>
    Carry the stable-yellow family into Derelicts and Gauntlet: larger surfaces,
    warm size colours and independent fields. The broader roster is deferred.
    <a href="#03">Readiness</a>
    · <a href="#04">Delivery scope</a>.
  </p>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Recognising what lies ahead</h2>
    <p>
      A pilot approaching the established standard Sun can collect energy while
      contending with gravitational pull, exposure and lethal contact. A
      different stellar nature should invite a different reading of the
      encounter: what is tempting, what threatens, and whether to approach, wait
      or leave. Colour, surface structure and activity around the body must make
      that character visible together, within the game’s high-contrast
      procedural art direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The subject is the stellar encounter’s visible identity and the approach
      it suggests. Candidates must differ in nature, rather than merely resize
      or recolour the Sun. A white giant, a blue dwarf and a violently active
      star are research prompts, not established categories or a selected
      roster. Each candidate needs a scientific basis connected to a proposed
      pilot experience and a concrete visual alternative. Observed phenomena,
      speculative possibilities and deliberate arcade abstractions must be
      distinguished. The tension is between an unfamiliar stellar character and
      cues a pilot can understand before committing to danger.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard Sun and current ship handling remain the reference. Candidate
      appearances, activity and encounter opportunities remain open; new
      mechanics are hypotheses until investigated. Visual comparisons must
      precede roster selection and numerical tuning. Close views establish
      surface character; flight-scale views and sequences establish what remains
      legible and how activity warns. These examples can test communication, but
      cannot establish survival balance. Generation, progression and changes to
      ship handling are adjacent questions outside this cycle. This cycle
      supersedes the earlier stellar-encounters inquiry for stellar identity;
      its open registry status does not make it a dependency or a second owner
      of this answer. Earlier spatial observations are limited evidence; its
      roster and delivery scope have no authority here.
    </p>
    <p class="question-check">
      Question test · 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass after correction and
      recheck; Grounded — Pass; Consequence — Pass.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/stellar-characters-2026-11-11"
        >Full review and ownership finding</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <details class="retained-round">
    <summary>02.1 / Four stellar natures and the retained direction</summary>
    <section>
      <h2 id="021">02.1 / Wind, eruption, wreckage, sweep</h2>
      <p>
        Four encounter hypotheses beside the unchanged Sun. Each plate has a
        close view and three smaller encounter frames, read left to right. The
        Wasp stays in place so the changing surroundings carry the comparison.
        These are procedural concept storyboards, not simulated approaches or
        proposed field boundaries. Body sizes are pictorial; the close views are
        enlarged independently. Neither sizes nor intervals specify game tuning.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Sun and Wasp use their production drawing functions. Candidate
        artwork is study-local. All sequences are static, deterministic and
        equally inspectable without animation. Surface detail may suggest a
        nature at close range; the smaller frames test which surrounding cues
        could carry it in flight. The interaction-led direction is retained
        below; no exact artwork, mechanic, numerical profile or complete roster
        is selected.
      </p>
      <h3>Reference / The steady bargain</h3>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/021-plate-0.png"
        alt="Standard Sun reference: close view and three steady compositions"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <p class="caption">
        Unchanged G artwork at illustrative zooms, sampled at 700, 1150 and 1600
        ms. Its rings are decorative, not warnings of timed damage. Collection,
        pull, exposure and contact retain the
        <a href="/lab/entities/star">standard Sun’s rules</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="candidate" id="white-supergiant">
      <p class="candidate-id">A / WHITE SUPERGIANT · OBSERVED NATURE</p>
      <h3>A luminous sea that pours outward</h3>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/021-plate-1.png"
        alt="White supergiant concept: bright surface and persistent radial outflow"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <div class="reading">
        <p>
          <strong>Basis.</strong>
          The white-giant prompt becomes a specific A-type supergiant, inspired
          by Deneb: an evolved luminous star with a stellar wind. Observations
          support mass outflow and time-dependent ejections, rather than a large
          copy of the Sun.
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2095"
            >Deneb and Rigel spectroscopy / interferometry</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
          Abundant light tempts a sustained collection pass, but the outflow
          makes lingering costly. Enter obliquely, take energy and turn away.
          The distinctive choice is exposure to a persistent hostile
          environment, rather than waiting for an eruption.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
          A pearl-white photosphere, fine luminous mottling and disconnected
          outward streaks. Their persistence should say “this environment stays
          hostile”. The cool-looking colour must not imply safety.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
        visible wind strokes, exaggerated surface texture, compressed distances
        and survivable harvesting. Wind is plasma, not air resistance; no
        outward push or propulsion benefit follows from this sketch. It does not
        predict collection yield or damage.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="candidate" id="flare-dwarf">
      <p class="candidate-id">B / ACTIVE RED DWARF · OBSERVED NATURE</p>
      <h3>A dim ember with a violent temper</h3>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/021-plate-2.png"
        alt="Active red dwarf concept: surface activity and widening bloom"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <div class="reading">
        <p>
          <strong>Basis.</strong>
          Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf with observed powerful flares. Low
          steady luminosity does not make an active small star benign.
          <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/exoplanets/neighboring-stars-bad-behavior-large-and-frequent-flares/"
            >NASA’s Proxima flare observations</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
          Surface activity and a broad luminous atmosphere suggest greater
          exposure reach than the other encounters. Collection may still tempt
          an approach, but the pilot should expect to keep more distance.
          Whether activity also creates collection windows remains open.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
          Dark patches and orange cells distinguish the surface. Scattered arcs
          and an encompassing bloom carry that activity beyond the body when
          surface detail disappears at flight scale. This replacement tests a
          broad exposure cue; its exact texture and bloom are provisional.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
        visible circumstellar bloom and its relation to a larger gameplay hazard
        region. The storyboard shows increasing activity, not an accepted
        warning timer or a measured damage boundary. Bloom does not establish a
        safe edge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="candidate" id="white-dwarf">
      <p class="candidate-id">
        C / DEBRIS-BEARING WHITE DWARF · OBSERVED NATURE
      </p>
      <h3>A hard light among the remains</h3>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/021-plate-3.png"
        alt="Debris-bearing white dwarf concept: hard light within moving remains"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <div class="reading">
        <p>
          <strong>Basis.</strong>
          A white dwarf is a dense stellar remnant, no longer powered by core
          fusion. Disrupted planetary material is observed around some white
          dwarfs; the debris belongs to this particular encounter, not the
          entire family.
          <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/resources/life-and-death/chapter-7/"
            >NASA on remnants</a
          >;
          <a
            href="https://astrobiology.nasa.gov/news/asteroid-remains-around-dead-stars-reveal-the-likely-fate-of-our-solar-system/"
            >observed planetary debris</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
          A small luminous target sits within a moving obstacle belt. Collect on
          the near side, watch fragments crossing the exit, and resist diving
          toward the point. Compact appearance must not imply a weak
          gravitational commitment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
          Almost featureless white glare contrasts with a broken, warm debris
          belt. The surroundings carry the character when the body is only a
          dot. A torn belt should read as material crossing a path, distinct
          from the Sun’s decorative rings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
        individually visible, widely spaced fragments, traversable gaps and
        survivable near-remnant flight. The belt’s projection and drift are
        illustrative. No salvage reward, collision law, mass or collection reach
        is established.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="candidate" id="pulsar">
      <p class="candidate-id">D / PULSAR · OBSERVED REMNANT</p>
      <h3>A lighthouse with no safe side forever</h3>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/021-plate-4.png"
        alt="Pulsar concept: a sweeping pair of beams"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <div class="reading">
        <p>
          <strong>Basis.</strong>
          A pulsar is a rotating neutron-star remnant whose radiation beams can
          sweep across an observer like a lighthouse. It is neither a blue Sun
          nor a periodically exploding star.
          <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/universe/stars/neutron-stars/pulsars/"
            >NASA on pulsars</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
          Follow a rotating gap or cross just after the sweep; keeping one
          distance no longer answers the danger. The first opportunity could be
          passage through the encounter rather than collection. Ordinary solar
          collectors’ response to the emission remains an unresolved fiction
          choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
          An almost point-like white core, sparse violet structure and two
          continuous opposing beams. Rotation, rather than an expanding circular
          pulse, carries the warning. The central surface is deliberately not
          the main recognition cue.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
        normally invisible emission made visible, a three-dimensional beam
        mapped into the play plane, a greatly slowed sweep and survivable gaps.
        The sketch establishes no radius beyond which the beam is safe.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>The blue-dwarf prompt / a different kind of uncertainty</h3>
      <p>
        Stellar-evolution calculations predict that some red dwarfs eventually
        become hotter blue dwarfs, on timescales longer than the current age of
        the Universe. This is a predicted future state, not an observed
        present-day population, and “blue” does not mean cold.
        <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/asna.200510440"
          >Adams, Bodenheimer and Laughlin’s evolution calculations</a
        >. It could belong in far-future fiction, but that basis alone supplies
        no distinctive surface activity or pilot decision. A blue-painted small
        Sun would not answer this study. Its encounter character remains
        unresolved; it is neither selected nor scientifically ruled out.
      </p>
      <h3>What this comparison can settle</h3>
      <p>
        These alternatives separate persistent outflow, broad stellar activity,
        moving material and a rotating sweep. Their colours support temperature
        and contrast; surface and surroundings carry the stronger distinctions.
        The saturated palettes, glare and exaggerated structure are graphic
        proposals, not naked-eye reconstructions of the objects.
      </p>
      <p>
        The plates make the proposed characters inspectable together. They do
        not establish that warnings are sufficient during a real approach, or
        that collection is worth the danger. A retained direction would next
        need its cue tested in a moving production flight composition with the
        actual HUD, ship, camera and competing effects. Field profiles and
        timing would follow that design evidence. No finding from the earlier
        spatial study selects any of these directions.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3 id="retained-direction">
        Retained direction / Light, reach and exposure must agree
      </h3>
      <p>
        Stellar variety changes how a star interacts with its surroundings, with
        visible identity communicating those differences. A colour substitution
        alone is insufficient. The standard Sun remains the established
        reference; its simple artwork does not limit the visual vocabulary of
        other natures. The comparison supports this direction, not acceptance of
        every depicted object or effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        The white supergiant’s abundant outward light suggests collection from
        farther away. The debris-bearing remnant suggests a smaller energy
        opportunity, while surface activity and atmospheric bloom suggest a
        larger exposure region for the active star. These relationships are
        intended game characters, not tuned profiles or measurements inferred
        from picture brightness. In particular, a hot white dwarf still
        radiates; compact size alone does not determine collector income. Exact
        yields and reaches remain open, and all cues must eventually agree with
        the actual fields.
      </p>
      <p>
        Surface differentiation and surrounding light are retained qualities for
        the active star. The local eruption drawing is replaced by the
        broad-bloom proposal above; the replacement’s appearance and event
        timing remain unaccepted. The pulsar remains optional. A vertical,
        bottom-to-top light axis with pulsing is a possible alternative to the
        rotating sweep, still requiring a concrete comparison. An intensity
        pulse on a fixed axis would be a deliberate arcade translation of pulsar
        emission, not the same phenomenon as a lighthouse beam crossing the
        observer.
      </p>
      <h3>One nature across World, FX and LFX</h3>
      <p>
        The encounter can use the three existing rendering layers together. A
        proposed division gives World the body, surface and physical
        surroundings; persistent FX the emitted light and fading aftermath; and
        LFX the current glare, bloom or active pulse. Their contributions should
        express the same stellar state. This does not require every star to use
        every layer, and fading history must not masquerade as a current hazard
        boundary. The plates remain single-canvas sketches; they do not
        demonstrate that composition in the production stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        Field geometry and collection strength already have limited supporting
        evidence from the earlier study. Repeating collection flights does not
        address the remaining uncertainty: which one additional stellar
        character complements the standard Sun without requiring a catalogue of
        variants. Exact artwork, field tuning and pulsar inclusion remain open.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details class="retained-round">
    <summary>02.2 / Surface, emitted light and a changing temperament</summary>
    <section>
      <h2 id="022">02.2 / One steady bargain, one changing temperament</h2>
      <p>
        The active star is the selected direction for this comparison, not yet
        an approved production star. This experiment adds time: a pilot can
        learn the standard Sun’s steady radial tradeoff, while the active star
        also asks whether this is a good moment to approach. That is an
        additional kind of difference, beyond the static contrast now
        prioritised below. The supergiant, debris-bearing remnant and pulsar
        remain available alternatives outside this focused round.
      </p>
      <p>
        The retained sequence samples four moments of the illustrative cycle.
        Both bodies have the same apparent radius and the same stationary Wasp
        alongside them, removing size as an explanation for the difference. The
        active surface gathers bright regions, short arcs intensify, and its
        luminous atmosphere spreads before subsiding. The Sun’s decorative rings
        continue throughout; they imply no change to its established hazard.
      </p>
      <div class="sequence">
        <figure>
          <img
            class="evidence"
            src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/022-quiet.png"
            alt="Quiet active star beside the steady Sun"
            loading="lazy"
          >
          <figcaption>Quiet · 1 s</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            class="evidence"
            src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/022-buildup.png"
            alt="Gathering hotspots and widening atmosphere"
            loading="lazy"
          >
          <figcaption>Buildup · 4.8 s</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            class="evidence"
            src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/022-peak.png"
            alt="Peak arcs and broad active-star bloom"
            loading="lazy"
          >
          <figcaption>Peak · 6.8 s</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            class="evidence"
            src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/022-recovery.png"
            alt="Activity subsides during recovery"
            loading="lazy"
          >
          <figcaption>Recovery · 10 s</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">
        Deterministic art fixture, sampled in four paused moments. Fixed view,
        1040 × 420 logical pixels, equal 46 px body radii, production Wasp
        drawings at flight scale. The illustrative 12 s cycle has Quiet at 0–3
        s, Buildup at 3–6 s, Peak at 6–8 s and Recovery at 8–12 s. These are
        inspection intervals, not accepted event timing. Retained moments are 1,
        4.8, 6.8 and 10 s, captured at closure on 11 November 2026; these are
        not earlier-run recordings.
      </p>
      <h3>What the activity should tell the pilot</h3>
      <p>
        Quiet is an invitation to consider approaching, not a claim of safety.
        Gathering surface light and a widening atmosphere propose “prepare to
        leave”; the peak proposes “keep away”; subsiding light permits a new
        assessment. Reliable buildup and an escape-length warning would be
        arcade inventions, rather than promises supplied by observed stellar
        flares. Whether higher activity enlarges exposure reach, increases
        damage, or both remains open. Collection does not automatically rise
        with the flash.
      </p>
      <h3>Surface, emitted light and present activity</h3>
      <p>
        This fixture uses the shared FlightLayers composition, including its FX
        bloom and layer blends, and the shared FxSurface for fading light. World
        carries the proposed surface; FX carries emitted arcs and their brief
        residue; LFX carries the present luminous atmosphere. Layer switches in
        the retired fixture exposed those contributions. Each captured moment
        rebuilt light history at 60 Hz, with the image held without adding
        light. The stellar painter is local to this study, and the retained 02.1
        plate remains a separate fixed variant.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example tests a changing character alongside the Sun, not approach
        survival. It runs no flight physics, collection, damage or candidate
        hazard boundaries, and contains no full flight HUD or competing combat
        effects. A bloom’s visible edge is not a damage edge. The remaining
        visual question is whether the buildup reads as a change of danger
        before the brightest moment, rather than as decoration or a promise of
        extra energy. The exact surface, light treatment, timing and production
        implementation are unaccepted.
      </p>
      <h3>Implication / Static contrasts before an activity cycle</h3>
      <p>
        The layered example usefully separates surface, emitted light and
        present luminosity, but an activity cycle exceeds the immediate need. A
        second stellar character can instead have constant fields with different
        relationships. Ordinary stars could keep similar body, hazard and
        collection proportions across their sizes, while gravity reach crosses
        from slightly inside collection for small stars to slightly outside it
        for large ones. That is a proposed family rule, not adopted gravity
        tuning or an established physical scaling law.
      </p>
      <p>
        A contrasting static encounter could offer little collectable energy yet
        remain dangerous across a broad region, possibly beyond its collection
        reach, with gravity defined independently. “Cold but dangerous” is a
        working character prompt; its scientific basis and temperature remain to
        be chosen. The useful contrast is the relationship of opportunity to
        danger, rather than a requirement for timed eruptions. Neither field
        ratios nor hazard extending beyond collection are settled. Existing
        spatial evidence is sufficient to motivate this comparison without
        another collection-flight experiment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Production currently draws the standard Sun’s body and decorative
        expanding rings together on World: the star pass in
        <code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/flight-frame.ts</code>
        calls
        <code>packages/core/src/lib/assets/star.ts</code>. The painter’s
        <code>glowColor</code>
        colours those strokes; it does not supply a separate FX bloom. The field
        HUD is also on World, in a separate pass. Separating the star’s emitted
        light from its body is therefore a distinct rendering question worth
        investigating even for steady stars; it requires no fluctuating gameplay
        fields. This study has not changed production rendering.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details class="retained-round">
    <summary>02.3 / Two steady families and their first size curves</summary>
    <section>
      <h2 id="023">02.3 / Two steady families, each with its own size curve</h2>
      <p>
        “Stable yellow” is a working game-family name, with the standard Sun as
        its reference, not a claim that one scientific stellar class spans this
        size range. The second family is defined first by its bargain: low
        collection yield and broad danger, with gravity independent of either.
        Its colour, name and scientific identity remain open. Surface and
        surrounding light should distinguish the two before their HUD lines
        explain the difference.
      </p>
      <p>
        The retained capture shows both stars at size 1 with the field HUD
        enabled. Both graphs show the full earlier proposed gradient, with a
        cursor at the inspected size. Body size means radius; every field reach
        is measured from the centre. The scenes share a fixed camera scale and
        fixed Wasp positions. Enlarged surface views retain the size
        relationship. The scene and graphs read the same profile function, so
        changing size changes the body, light, HUD and numeric readouts
        together.
      </p>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/023-two-families.png"
        alt="Earlier yellow and low-yield hypotheses at size one, HUD enabled, with radius and gravity-strength curves"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <h3>Starting curves / explicit hypotheses</h3>
      <p>
        Size runs from 0.5 to 2 times the reference radius. In the yellow
        family, body, hazard and collection radii grow linearly; gravity reach
        grows with size to the power 1.12. Size 1 retains the standard Sun’s
        radius, field reaches and peak collection. Gravity therefore ends a
        little inside collection at the small end and a little outside it at the
        large end. This is a proposed game relationship, not an astrophysical
        scaling law.
      </p>
      <p>
        The contrasting family starts with the same body radius, 42% of the
        Sun’s collection reach, 155% of its hazard reach and 90% of its gravity
        reach. Their size exponents are respectively 1, 0.85, 1.05 and 1.25. The
        chosen example keeps peak collection at 2 E/s per collector-equivalent,
        versus the yellow family’s 7.5 E/s. These values make the proposed
        relationship inspectable; none is accepted tuning. At some sizes the
        hazard exceeds collection, meaning that collecting would require
        exposure. That consequence is visible, not a settled requirement for the
        family.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gravity strength is shown separately at a fixed 100 u from the centre.
        The study derives a hypothetical mass for each proposed reach using the
        existing gravity law and cutoff; it changes no gravity constants or
        production settings. The shared mass-law functions produce reach and
        pull. No ship trajectory or survival outcome is inferred from the
        graphs.
      </p>
      <h3>Appearance before taxonomy</h3>
      <p>
        Both examples deliberately stay near the warm palette. The yellow family
        keeps its smooth body and decorative rings, with a proposed steady LFX
        halo. The contrasting family proposes a mottled surface and broken
        circumstellar light through persistent FX and its shared bloom. This
        tests whether structure and luminous extent can carry identity without
        relying on blue versus yellow. Neither the palette nor these exact layer
        assignments are selected. The new halo is a study proposal, not current
        production Sun artwork.
      </p>
      <p class="caption">
        Static deterministic fixture: 1040 × 470 logical pixels, 0.28 px/u, no
        random seed, Sun artwork sampled at 900 ms. FX reconstructs a steady
        half-second history through the shared FxSurface; no cycle runs. World,
        FX and LFX use shared FlightLayers. HUD lines use the proposed profile
        geometry: the production Sun HUD still assumes coincident collection and
        gravity reaches and cannot express this comparison directly. This sketch
        runs no collection, damage or physics simulation. Light extent is a cue,
        not an exact field boundary; the revealed HUD supplies those boundaries.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <section>
    <h2 id="024">02.4 / The yellow star beside a firing ship</h2>
    <p>
      The earlier intended roster was two families: stable yellows, with the Sun
      as their reference, and low-yields. Their size gradients are a retained
      direction; 02.3’s exact curves remain proposals. The low-yield’s final
      identity and appearance are open, including whether any qualities of the
      hard light among remains belong to it. The closing decision takes the
      yellow family forward alone; low-yields remain outside delivery.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outward stellar rings should be judged beside something they can cross.
      This comparison holds a real Wasp near the Sun, with its ion engine
      firing. The left scene retains production rendering. The right keeps the
      same yellow body on World and moves the existing outward rings to LFX,
      above the ship and accumulated engine light. The two retained moments show
      the rings at different positions beside the same firing ship. These are
      composition samples, not a flight trajectory.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/024-layers-1_5s.png"
        alt="Production World-only star beside accepted World body, FX corona and LFX rings, both next to a firing Wasp at 1.5 seconds"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Layer composition reference · 1.5 s, radius 35, ship distance 85 u.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <details>
      <summary>Second moment / live rings cross the ship</summary>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/024-layers-2_5s.png"
        alt="Same layer comparison at 2.5 seconds, with rings in their new positions"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <p class="caption">
        Same seed, fixed ship and layer settings; inspection time 2.5 s. This
        pair retains two positions, not a continuous recording of smoothness or
        persistence.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3>Live rings first; persistent glow as a separate choice</h3>
    <p>
      LFX redraws the rings at their current positions. They cross the ship
      without leaving old circles behind. The optional steady FX corona adds a
      cached warm glow through the same persistent light surface as the engine.
      The combined World body, FX corona and LFX rings are the accepted
      yellow-star composition reference. The World-only view is the baseline;
      only the right scene with all three layers visible is accepted. Its
      radius-35 surface is superseded by the enlarged 02.5 family.
    </p>
    <h3 id="accepted-yellow">Accepted / A solid body inside light</h3>
    <p>
      The warm corona gives the yellow body a luminous presence, while the
      outward rings cross a nearby ship and its engine light instead of sitting
      behind them. This combination supplies the standard-size composition
      reference; 02.5 subsequently settles the family’s larger surface
      proportions. The exact retained variant is the right scene with rings set
      to LFX, steady FX corona enabled, and World, FX and LFX all visible. Its
      default inspection moment remains 1.5 s, ship distance 85 u and rotation
      off; those are replay conditions, not gameplay constraints.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retain the existing ring effect while the family is explored. It works in
      the accepted composition; acceptance does not claim that circles are the
      only or best possible stellar rays. A little irregularity or breathing in
      the corona remains optional future visual exploration. Steady light is
      sufficient for the accepted reference, and cosmetic variation would not
      imply fluctuating collection, danger or gravity. The low-yield appearance
      is still open; it should be investigated against this yellow reference.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both scenes use the production Simulation, engine output, ship painter,
      FlightFx, ordered frame passes and FlightLayers composition. The right
      scene splits the existing star painter with body and outside-body clips;
      it invents no second formula for the rings. The corona alone is new study
      artwork. Production stars, field HUD and gameplay are unchanged.
      Decorative rings do not indicate an expanding damage boundary.
    </p>
    <p class="caption">
      Retained held-pose rendering fixture at 1.5 and 2.5 s, captured at
      closure. Seed 2401, default Wasp, both thrust inputs held, no weapons,
      Practice resource protection, encounters off and planets removed. Ship
      position and velocity are staged before every production 1/60 s tick.
      Initial centre-distance 85 u, selectable 60–150 u; heading π, optional
      rotation 0.45 rad/s. Each view is 520 × 440 logical pixels, zoom 2, camera
      centred at (990, 540); Sun at (960, 540), radius 35. Seeking rebuilds
      engine and light history. The engine readout reports delivered output.
      This shows composition and overlap, not flight handling, exposure cost or
      escape.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="025">02.5 / A warm size range with a stronger gravity contrast</h2>
    <p>
      The yellow family is accepted on 11 November 2026. Their World body, FX
      corona and LFX rings supply the retained composition. The relative size
      range, warm palette and gravity-reach exponent 1.45 are retained
      directions. The remaining proportion was the surface against the ship: all
      three bodies looked too small. This revision doubles surface radii while
      preserving every field’s centre-distance radius and gravity mass. The
      enlarged variant is the final visual reference; production delivery
      remains separate work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The revised surface radii are 36 / 70 / 140 u, replacing 18 / 35 / 70 u.
      These are radii, so their diameters are 72 / 140 / 280 u. Collection
      remains 167 / 324 / 648 u and hazard remains 51 / 100 / 200 u from the
      centre; gravity retains its previous reach and pull at each family size.
      Fields do not move outward with the surface. The wider control limits were
      inspection bounds; the accepted initial surface range is 36–140 u. The
      same firing Wasp provides a size reference in every view. The small end
      should remain recognisable as a star beside the ship; the large end should
      feel imposing without losing the ability to read its body and surrounding
      light together.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/025-accepted-family.png"
        alt="Accepted yellow family: surface radii 36, 70 and 140 beside the same Wasp, with complete HUD field overviews and fixed field dimensions"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Accepted variant · doubled surfaces, warm colour enabled, exponent 1.45.
        The control positions in this retained capture identify the inspected
        settings.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        class="evidence"
        src="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/025-accepted-curves.png"
        alt="Accepted radius and outer collection pull curves, compared with the earlier exponent 1.12"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        The same family profile drives both graphs. The hard gravity cutoff
        causes the jump in the pull graph.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="caption">
      <a
        href="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/025-before-surface-enlargement.png"
        >Earlier surface comparison</a
      >: radii 18 / 35 / 70 u, exponent 1.45, warm colour enabled, the same
      ship, field boundaries and camera scales. The larger surfaces reduce the
      gap to the unchanged hazard boundary: the revised standard Sun has 30 u
      between its radius-70 surface and radius-100 hazard reach.
    </p>
    <h3>
      Make smaller collection gentler and larger collection more committed
    </h3>
    <p>
      Surface, hazard and collection radii grow linearly within the revised
      family; the surface alone is twice its earlier size. The retained starting
      gravity-reach exponent is 1.45, versus 1.12 in 02.3. Both curves meet the
      reference fields at the revised Sun surface radius 70. At the default
      small end, gravity reaches about 74% of collection; at the large end,
      about 137%. The retained exponent changes the family’s mass scaling
      without changing the gravity force law or cutoff constant.
    </p>
    <p>
      A second graph samples pull at 80% of each star’s collection reach. This
      compares the same relative place to collect, rather than the same absolute
      distance from differently sized bodies. The proposed small star has an
      outer collection region beyond gravity’s cutoff; the large star pulls
      harder there than under 02.3’s curve. That is a concrete opportunity and
      commitment difference. It does not by itself establish survival difficulty
      or net energy gain; collection rates, propulsion and hazard still matter.
    </p>
    <h3>One warm family, with variation across size</h3>
    <p>
      The proposed palette goes from deeper warm orange at the small end,
      through the established Sun amber at surface radius 70, toward pale gold
      at the large end. The body, ring tint and cached corona change together;
      the composition stays World + FX + LFX. The comparison also isolated size
      by turning colour variation off; the accepted variant keeps it on. This is
      an arcade family cue, not an adopted relationship between physical radius
      and stellar temperature, and it introduces no blue or separate stellar
      taxonomy.
    </p>
    <p class="caption">
      Fixed 1.5 s inspection moment, seed 2401, default Wasp with both thrust
      inputs held, Practice protection, no encounters or planets. Each panel
      used the study’s 02.4 light rig, zoom 1, the same 520 × 440 logical view
      and camera centre (990, 540). The additional HUD overviews centre on the
      star at (960, 540), share a scale fitted to the largest proposed field,
      and use the shared HUD painter with the proposed field radii. Their
      inactive rings show geometry rather than collection or damage activity.
      Ship centre-distance remains 44 / 85 / 170 u at the default sizes (85/70
      times the revised surface radius); the ship keeps its production size. The
      corona and decorative rays follow the enlarged body, while HUD field
      boundaries retain their previous radii. Candidate gravity masses are
      derived with the existing law. Production collection/exposure profiles are
      not replaced in the held rendering fixture; the graphs describe the
      proposed family, not a resource-limited simulation. These captures
      establish the accepted initial family limits, not production tuning or
      verified gameplay balance. 02.3 remains separately available.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="03">03 / One accepted family, with separate fields</h2>
    <p>
      This study closes <strong>accepted</strong>, with a positive design
      assessment. The useful result is a coherent stable-yellow / G-inspired
      game family: different size, colour and gravitational commitment,
      expressed through a solid World body, warm persistent FX corona and
      outward LFX rings. The enlarged surfaces beside the unchanged ship resolve
      the final proportion concern. “G-class” is the game-family shorthand here;
      this arcade gradient is not a physical law for real G spectral-class
      stars.
    </p>
    <p>
      The opening sought substantially different stellar natures. Exploration
      showed that static field relationships and a better yellow reference
      already supply a valuable, manageable delivery. The closing decision
      intentionally narrows the result to that family. Low-yields, white
      supergiants, debris-bearing remnants, pulsars and activity cycles are
      retained ideas, not an accepted roster or delivery dependency. The earlier
      failed study’s proposed scope does not return through this closure.
    </p>
    <h3>Accepted dimensions and visible identity</h3>
    <p>
      Let <code>s = surface radius / 70</code>, with an initial range
      <code>36/70 ≤ s ≤ 2</code>. Surface radius is <code>70s</code>, hazard
      radius <code>100s</code>, collection radius <code>R₀s</code> and gravity
      radius <code>R₀s^1.45</code>, where <code>R₀ ≈ 324.037 u</code> is the
      existing reference gravity reach. All reaches are measured from the
      centre. Doubling the surface does not add that increase to any field. With
      the current inverse-square law, mass is <code>5250s^2.9</code>; falloff,
      gravitational constant and cutoff remain unchanged.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Initial example</th>
          <th>Surface radius</th>
          <th>Hazard</th>
          <th>Collection</th>
          <th>Gravity</th>
          <th>Pull at 0.8 × collection</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Small</th>
          <td>36 u</td>
          <td>51 u</td>
          <td>167 u</td>
          <td>123.5 u</td>
          <td>0 u/s²</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Reference</th>
          <td>70 u</td>
          <td>100 u</td>
          <td>324 u</td>
          <td>324 u</td>
          <td>78.1 u/s²</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Large</th>
          <td>140 u</td>
          <td>200 u</td>
          <td>648 u</td>
          <td>885 u</td>
          <td>145.8 u/s²</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Small stars leave outer collection space beyond gravity’s cutoff. Large
      stars pull before collection begins and pull harder at the same relative
      collection distance. The enlarged standard surface leaves 30 u before the
      hazard boundary. These are geometric and force-law consequences, not
      measured survival difficulty or net energy gain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep the accepted deeper orange → amber → pale gold gradient, with the
      reference amber at radius 70; body, decorative ring tint and corona vary
      together. World HUD retains solid green collection, dashed green gravity
      and dotted amber danger, including distinct independent boundaries. The
      complete colour anchors, interpolation and numeric samples are retained in
      <a href="/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11/accepted-reference.json"
        >the reference data</a
      >. Cosmetic corona breathing is optional later work; no timed gameplay
      field or new light-ray design is required.
    </p>
    <h3>Reusable systems and required adaptations</h3>
    <p>
      Reuse the shared star painter, FlightLayers, ordered flight-frame passes,
      FlightFx persistence, cached emission stamps and existing mass-law
      functions. The study’s body/rings clipping demonstrates layering;
      production should expose body and ring drawing through their shared owner
      rather than maintain a separate study renderer. The shared HUD painter
      already supports independent proposed hazard and gravity radii.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production still creates one radius-35 G Sun for every mode. In
      <code>setStarMassWell</code>, <code>influenceRadius</code> follows
      gravity;
      <code>solarField</code>
      reads that same value as collection reach.
      <code>starPull</code>
      also uses it as the gravity gate. Damage uses the global standard-Sun
      profile. Separate validated per-star collection, exposure and mass-well
      definitions are therefore required before these pictures can become
      gameplay. Stock ships, assembled builds, derelicts and Gauntlet actors
      must consume the same definitions, not HUD-only radii.
    </p>
    <p>
      The accepted result settles field boundaries, surface scaling and visible
      identity. It does not settle the collection/exposure shoulder or peaks,
      size distribution, or encounter placement. Enlarging the body changes
      collision clearance and can place the old radius-55 shoulder below its
      surface. A conservative implementation proposal preserves the existing
      centre-distance profile scaled by <code>s</code>, including shoulder
      <code>55s</code>, peaks 7.5 E/s and 1.5 hull/s and collection edge
      fraction 0.1; this remains a proposal requiring an explicit implementation
      decision, not an inference from the held-pose artwork. Do not silently
      attach a new shoulder to the enlarged surface.
    </p>
    <h3>Modes, Playground and the challenge boundary</h3>
    <p>
      The accepted mode boundary is <strong>Derelicts and Gauntlet</strong>.
      Recommended first delivery: select a deterministic size within 36–140 u
      from the run seed in those modes, using an isolated random stream so the
      selection does not perturb other encounters. The sampling distribution is
      still provisional; it is not a new player tuning control. Keep the Sun
      steady for the whole run. Survival and Practice retain their existing Sun,
      physics and rendering; the decorative landing system also stays on its
      existing definition.
    </p>
    <p>
      Playground currently unlocks ships and parts and disables scoring while
      retaining the selected mode’s simulation. The recommendation is to keep
      that meaning: Derelicts and Gauntlet get the family with Playground Off or
      On; switching Playground does not independently change Survival or
      Practice stars. Extending Playground into a world-feature switch remains
      an unresolved product choice outside this first scope. This recommendation
      does not record that extension as approved.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app version and leaderboard challenge identifier are independent. With
      scored Survival untouched, this scope alone supplies no reason to rotate
      its challenge or change score records and Firestore rules. Revisit that
      decision if Survival rules change. A release such as 0.7 can communicate
      the gameplay addition, but the release number is not selected here and
      this study changes no version or challenge identifier.
    </p>
    <h3>Evidence and limits</h3>
    <p>
      The retained 02.5 composition is the accepted final variant: radii 36 / 70
      / 140, exponent 1.45, warm colour enabled, World + FX + LFX visible, HUD
      shown. Seed 2401, default Wasp, both thrust inputs held, fixed 1.5 s
      moment, Practice resource protection, no planets or encounters. Close-ups
      share zoom 1; complete overviews share a scale fitted to the largest
      gravity field. Ship centres remain about 44 / 85 / 170 u from the star.
      Captures were made at closure, 11 November 2026; they preserve the
      selected conditions rather than proving earlier states.
    </p>
    <p>
      The designer accepted the visual proportions, colours, field relationships
      and gravity curve and judged the study successful. Earlier numerical and
      visual checks establish rendering and type correctness separately from
      that judgment. The examples held the ship in place and did not substitute
      candidate resource profiles: no resource balance, escape success, AI
      navigation, collision safety or performance in combat was established. Two
      02.4 moments retain ring positions; continuous motion quality has no
      retained recording. The four 02.2 moments preserve a proposed cycle, not
      accepted warning timing.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="04">04 / Deliver the yellow family in two modes</h2>
    <p>
      No change to <code>docs/design.md</code> principles is proposed. This is
      accepted implementation scope, not production delivery. Build the shared
      definitions first, then deliver the family in Derelicts; add Gauntlet
      using that same implementation. Low-yields and the wider stellar roster
      remain separate future design work.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Shared family profile and canonical Lab reference — medium.</strong
        >
        Define Zod contracts before adding per-star data. Put surface, colour,
        independent energy/exposure fields and mass-well configuration in shared
        owners in core and mode-a; preserve the legacy Sun as the fallback.
        Resolve the profile-shape proposal above. Extend the shared star painter
        and flight-frame composition for World body, FX corona and LFX rings;
        demonstrate the accepted three sizes at <code>/lab/entities/star</code>
        using those production definitions. Update the star, gravity and render
        specs. Verify exact centre-distance boundaries, palette anchors, field
        samples, cutoff semantics, independent HUD lines and legacy defaults. No
        live mode opts in yet; this slice is independently inspectable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Derelicts opt-in — medium, after slice 1.</strong>
        Select and retain one seeded family size for each run in Simulation,
        with no mid-run variation. Wire collection, hull damage, gravity and
        terminal contact for player and encounter actors to the shared profile.
        Review spawn, arrival, orbit and salvage-clearance assumptions against
        the radius-140 extreme. Update the Derelicts, systems and star specs;
        verify repeated seeds, minimum/reference/maximum cases, frame-rate
        independence and absence of changes in Survival, Practice and landing.
        Check keyboard and landscape touch play. This yields the first playable
        delivery without waiting for new star types.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gauntlet opt-in — small to medium, after slice 2.</strong>
        Reuse the same selection and profile, ensuring pirate pilots and all
        ships read the same surface and fields. Verify arrival separation, pilot
        avoidance, death/result handling and restart determinism across the
        three reference sizes. Update the Gauntlet spec. Preserve Playground
        scoring semantics and confirm the recommended mode matrix before
        shipping; expanding Playground’s purpose is a separate decision.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Release verification — small, after each playable slice.</strong
        >
        Run affected physics/resource/seed tests, type and contract gates and
        release smoke. Inspect the shared Lab and target modes for engine/star
        overlap, independent HUD boundaries, FX budget and camera jumps at the
        two size extremes. Report automated checks and visual inspection
        separately. Check that ranked Survival remains on its existing Sun and
        challenge; select release numbering through the normal release process.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Estimates are relative work sizes, not schedules. No production gameplay,
      global gravity/friction tuning, package version, score schema, challenge
      or deployment changes are included in this closure. Temporary interactive
      study apparatus has been replaced by these versioned captures and written
      definitions; shared game, layer, HUD and Lab systems remain.
    </p>
  </section>
